// phpup-2026 - Microsoft Sentinel KQL Detection Queries
// CVE-2026-109618 / CVE-2026-109619 · PHP longjmp/bailout state corruption · RCE
//
// 2026-10-21 rewrite. The prior revision keyed on a single-request
// multipart-stall heap-spray mechanism (oversized body, forged
// zend_fcall_info byte markers, 100+ identically-named array fields)
// derived from the leaked PoC artifact's own claims. That mechanism does
// not match the real fix (php/php-src commit 82f8f31b16a, "Avoid state
// corruption via bailout") and those queries are retired below, not
// caveated. See ANALYSIS.md for the full mechanism writeup.
//
// 2026-10-21, same day, second rewrite: every AzureDiagnostics query in
// this file (T2-1, T2-3, T3-3, T3-4, H1, H2) was built on a field,
// `requestBody_s`, that does not exist. This was caught on the
// Azure-Sentinel PR this file's queries were ported into
// (Azure/Azure-Sentinel#15377) by a maintainer review (@oferInbar)
// reporting a live SEM0100 schema error, then independently confirmed
// here against Microsoft's own schema reference
// (learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/reference/tables/agwfirewalllogs):
// AGWFirewallLogs (what AzureDiagnostics draws Application
// Gateway/Front Door WAF data from) has no raw-request-body column at
// all. The closest field, DetailedData, only contains a snippet when a
// managed ruleset rule has already matched something - not a
// general-purpose field a custom query can pattern-match against. This
// is not fixable by renaming a field; the data these six queries
// needed was never exposed by the log source. Retired below, not
// patched a third time. The duplicate-header-name OOM-amplification
// signal this file could not carry over Sentinel/AzureDiagnostics is
// still available via Suricata (rules/suricata.rules SID 9109633),
// which inspects the real HTTP body directly and has been live-fire
// verified against real captures - see that file and examples/INDEX.md.
//
// The real mechanism is cross-request: a longjmp-interrupted (producer)
// request leaves worker-persistent globals dangling; a later, different
// (consumer) request on the SAME WORKER reads them before its own init
// has run. "Same worker" is not visible from any of these log sources -
// none of them carry a worker/process identifier tied to the request.
//
// 2026-10-21, same day, third rewrite: checked every remaining field in
// this file against Microsoft's own schema references (same technique
// that caught requestBody_s above), not just the ones already under
// suspicion. Found one more: W3CIISLog has no `csHeaders` column either
// (learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/reference/tables/
// w3ciislog) - standard W3C IIS logging doesn't capture request headers
// beyond the specific fields that table lists. T1-1 fixed by dropping
// the clause that relied on it (now weaker: can no longer tell a
// multipart POST from any other POST); T2-2 retired outright, since
// that field was its entire basis for the "multipart" check and nothing
// is left without it. Confirmed real and unaffected: CommonSecurityLog's
// Message/RequestMethod/SourceIP/DestinationHostName/RequestURL
// (learn.microsoft.com/.../commonsecuritylog), DeviceProcessEvents'
// InitiatingProcessFileName/FileName/ProcessCommandLine/AccountName/
// DeviceName and TimeGenerated specifically - the latter isn't in the
// native M365 Defender Advanced Hunting schema (which calls it
// `Timestamp`), but is confirmed correct for Sentinel-ingested
// DeviceProcessEvents by cross-checking a real, already-merged
// detection in this same repo (Detections/DeviceProcessEvents/
// SolarWinds_SUNBURST_Process-IOCs.yaml uses `timestamp = TimeGenerated`
// directly) - and SecurityEvent's EventID/SubjectUserName/
// NewProcessName/CommandLine/ParentProcessName/Computer
// (learn.microsoft.com/.../securityevent). One real, non-schema finding
// while cross-checking against that same SUNBURST rule: this repo's
// PHPShellSpawn.yaml mapped the FileHash entity to DeviceProcessEvents'
// SHA256 column, which Microsoft's own schema docs say is "usually not
// populated - use the SHA1 column when available"; fixed there (see
// rules/sentinel/analytics-rules/phpup2026-PHPShellSpawn.yaml).
//
// 2026-10-21, fourth pass: checked the remaining queries' logic against
// real field values, the same real-data discipline already applied to
// Suricata/Sigma/YARA, using scripts/kql_eval.py (no live Log Analytics
// workspace available, so this verifies logic against real captured
// data rather than executing live KQL). T1-1 confirmed to match on this
// repo's own real benign/stall captures (translated to W3CIISLog field
// names) and correctly reject a constructed no-cookie negative. T3-1
// confirmed to match on the real shell_exec() capture and - unlike
// Sigma's process-spawn.yml, which documents the cron-under-www-data
// case as an expected false positive - correctly does NOT fire on the
// real cron capture, since this query requires InitiatingProcessFileName
// to match the parent-process regex with no account-based fallback; an
// intentional, more conservative design than Sigma's, not a bug. T3-2
// does share Sigma's cron false positive (has an account-based OR
// branch), but testing surfaced a real, independent gap: its
// NewProcessName list was missing "dash"/"zsh"/"ash"/"ncat"/"nmap",
// present in T3-1's identical-purpose list - a shell invoked directly
// as /bin/dash, with no "/bin/sh" substring anywhere in CommandLine,
// would have matched neither clause and been missed entirely. Fixed by
// syncing the list with T3-1's (and added "ash" to both, matching
// analytics-rules/phpup2026-PHPShellSpawn.yaml's already-more-complete
// list, which this file's copies had drifted from).
//
// Table coverage (after the third rewrite):
//   W3CIISLog              IIS web server logs
//   CommonSecurityLog      WAF / proxy / firewall (CEF)
//   DeviceProcessEvents    MDE process creation
//   SecurityEvent          Windows Security (EventID 4688)


// ── TIER 1: FEATURE PRESENCE (precondition, not an attack signal alone) ──────

// [T1-1] session.upload_progress feature reachable and in use - IIS
// Baseline precondition for the CVE-2026-109619 consumer path specifically.
// Also how the legitimate progress-bar feature is used.
// 2026-10-21 fix: this previously also checked `csHeaders has
// "multipart/form-data"` - confirmed against Microsoft's own W3CIISLog
// schema reference (learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/
// reference/tables/w3ciislog) that no `csHeaders` column exists at all;
// standard W3C IIS logging does not capture arbitrary request headers
// (including Content-Type), only the specific fields listed there. Not
// fixable by renaming a field. Removed that clause - this query can
// only use what the schema actually has (method, cookie, and the query
// string, for the rare case the field name leaks into a URL) and can no
// longer distinguish a multipart POST from any other POST. Weaker than
// before, honestly, not just differently worded.
W3CIISLog
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)
| where csMethod == "POST"
| where csCookie has "PHPSESSID=" or csUriQuery has "PHP_SESSION_UPLOAD_PROGRESS"
| project TimeGenerated, cIP, csHost, csUriStem, csMethod, csBytes, scStatus


// [T1-2] session.upload_progress feature in use - CommonSecurityLog (WAF/proxy)
// Vendor-dependent: whether a CEF-emitting WAF/proxy populates Message
// with request content (rather than just a rule-match summary) varies
// by product - verify against your own vendor's CEF mapping before
// relying on this.
CommonSecurityLog
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)
| where RequestMethod == "POST"
| where Message has "PHP_SESSION_UPLOAD_PROGRESS" and Message has "PHPSESSID="
| project TimeGenerated, SourceIP, DestinationHostName, RequestURL, Message


// ── TIER 3: BEHAVIORAL / POST-EXPLOITATION ───────────────────────────────────

// [T3-1] Shell spawned under PHP/web server process - MDE
// Post-exploitation indicator, generically valid regardless of which
// mechanism caused the RCE. Parent: php-fpm, php, lsphp, httpd/apache2
// (mod_php - PHP runs in-process inside the Apache worker, the SAPI
// CVE-2026-109618 was actually demonstrated against), nginx.
// 2026-10-21 fix: FileName switched from has_any (substring) to in~
// (exact, case-insensitive) - has_any on short tokens like "sh"/"id"/"nc"
// matches unrelated binaries (openssh, cloud-init, systemd-networkd).
// Caught by the same Azure-Sentinel PR review that found the
// requestBody_s schema error above; applied the same fix here.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)
| where InitiatingProcessFileName matches regex @"(?i)^(php-fpm[\d.]*|php[\d.]*|lsphp[\d]*|apache2|httpd|nginx)$"
| where FileName in~ ("sh", "bash", "dash", "zsh", "ash", "python", "python2", "python3", "perl", "curl", "wget", "nc", "ncat", "nmap")
       or ProcessCommandLine has_any ("/bin/sh", "/bin/bash", "bash -i", "python -c", "exec(", "eval(")
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, AccountName,
          InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine,
          FileName, ProcessCommandLine


// [T3-2] Shell spawned under web service account - SecurityEvent 4688
// For environments not running MDE. Requires process creation auditing
// enabled (Security Policy: Audit Process Creation + command-line in audit).
// 2026-10-21 fix: same has_any -> exact-match fix as T3-1, same reason.
// 2026-10-21, later the same day: building a real-data KQL evaluator
// (scripts/kql_eval.py) and testing it against this repo's own real
// shell-spawn capture surfaced that NewProcessName's list here was
// missing "dash"/"zsh"/"ncat"/"nmap", present in T3-1's identical-
// purpose list - a real gap, not theoretical: a shell invoked directly
// as /bin/dash (no "/bin/sh" substring anywhere in CommandLine) would
// have matched neither clause and been missed entirely, confirmed by
// isolating that exact case. Synced the list with T3-1's.
SecurityEvent
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)
| where EventID == 4688
| where SubjectUserName has_any ("www-data", "apache", "nobody", "lsphp", "iis apppool")
| where NewProcessName in~ ("sh", "bash", "dash", "zsh", "ash", "python", "python2", "python3", "curl", "wget", "nc", "ncat", "nmap")
       or CommandLine has_any ("/bin/sh", "/bin/bash", "bash -i", "python -c")
| project TimeGenerated, Computer, SubjectUserName,
          ParentProcessName, NewProcessName, CommandLine


// ── RETIRED (2026-10-21) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Old T1-3/T1-4/T1-5 (oversized body as spray proxy, dangerous-function
//   string in body, forged zend_fcall_info/zval byte markers): built on
//   the dead single-request spray mechanism. The byte-marker query in
//   particular came entirely from the leaked PoC artifact's own code,
//   which VulnCheck reports does not have the correct mechanism.
// Old T2-4 (oversized-body + 200/502/503 crash-ratio): the crash premise
//   was already flagged unvalidated in the prior revision (a real lab
//   test produced a clean 200 OK under the documented precondition) and
//   the premise itself (same-request stall causing a same-request crash)
//   is now known to be the wrong mechanism entirely.
// Old H2 (100+ identically-named array fields): built on the dead
//   spray-count theory.
// T2-1, T2-3, T3-3, T3-4, H1, H2 (duplicate/empty-file-field header
//   signals, burst correlation, hunting queries - all AzureDiagnostics):
//   retired 2026-10-21, second rewrite, same day as the fixes above.
//   All six queried a field, `requestBody_s`, that does not exist on
//   AzureDiagnostics/AGWFirewallLogs - see this file's header comment
//   for the schema verification. Not a logic bug fixable by patching;
//   the underlying data was never available from this log source.
// T2-2 (high header density in multipart body, W3CIISLog size proxy):
//   retired 2026-10-21, third rewrite, same day. Same root cause as the
//   W3CIISLog `csHeaders` fix on T1-1 above, but here the field being
//   removed (`csHeaders has "multipart/form-data"`) was this query's
//   ENTIRE basis for distinguishing a multipart POST from any other
//   POST - there is nothing left to salvage. W3CIISLog genuinely has no
//   way to tell whether a POST was multipart/form-data at all.
